{
  "schema": "chronolog-dataset/0",
  "dataset": "elder-scrolls",
  "title": "The Elder Scrolls, Merethic Era to the Fourth Era",
  "notes": [
    "NOT LOADED. This is the acceptance fixture for era support. Every coordinate here is an era coordinate, and no year on this calendar is a year on any other one.",
    "ERAS ARE FRAMES STAPLED TOGETHER. Each era is its own frame: it owns its year numbering (`firstYear`, `direction`) and its extent (`years`), and inherits its month/day structure from `frame:tamriel-calendar` by basis. The boundary between two consecutive eras is a `succession` staple whose two ends carry the whole of its meaning through their roles -- `end` names the era that finishes, `start` names the era that begins. There is no coordinate on that staple because there is nothing to author: the boundary IS where the earlier era's extent runs out.",
    "An event's placement names the ERA FRAME and a plain year. The frame it is attached to is which era it means, so no coordinate carries an era value.",
    "Merethic years DESCEND: ME 2500 is older than ME 1000, and ME 1 is the year immediately before 1E 1. `direction` says which way a given era's numbers run.",
    "Only an INTERIOR era needs a closed length, because that is the only thing anything has to count across. The first era in the chain may be open below it and the last open above it, and both of these are.",
    "Exactly one era carries the pin (`era.anchor`): the First Era states that its year 1 is proper year 1, and every other era's range derives from that plus the chain. Two pins would be two facts that can disagree.",
    "The months are Gregorian-length and there are twelve of them, so the month and day arithmetic IS the Gregorian family's -- `gregorian.months` and `gregorian.days`, with Tamrielic NAMES. A separate `tamriel.*` transition for identical arithmetic would be a second mechanism for one thing.",
    "`origin` and `clock: false` together: the origin is an arbitrary but STATED anchor, so the chain resolves to exact ordinals, and `clock: false` says this calendar claims no relation to the running clock -- so no artificial Now line is drawn on it. Whether that arbitrary anchor is the right one is a decision for the owner.",
    "Morndas is aligned to the Gregorian Monday, which is a declared convention and not a lore claim: cycle `offset` is the cycle index of day zero.",
    "Where the lore gives only a year, the coordinate stops at year. Merethic entries carry the `approximate` trait because Merethic year numbers are not attested.",
    "The Dawn Era is a frame with NO metric ladder: no basis, `countable: false`. It is ordered and connected -- it is in the succession chain -- and never acquires day ordinals, time there being the thing that had not started behaving yet. Its events hold their sequence and answer no question about when."
  ],
